    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The neuropeptide oxytocin plays a role in generating social behavior appropriate to context. Although pharmacological studies have implicated the peptide in a wide range of behaviors, oxytocin knockout studies in mice have shown that the peptide is not necessary for many of its associated behaviors. Mice, however, have a limited behavioral repertoire and cannot alone provide a complete picture of the peptide’s necessity in regulating various social behaviors. In this study, we used the cichlid fish Astatotilapia burtoni to characterize the social behavior role of isotocin, the oxytocin homolog found in teleost fish. Females of this species perform unique reproductive behaviors, whereas males adapt their social status to match their changing social environment. We first analyzed the role of isotocin in female spawning behavior by studying a line of A. burtoni carrying a CRISPR-generated null mutation in the isotocin gene (IT-/-). We then described the activity of isotocin neurons by quantifying the coexpression of isotocin with egr-1, an immediate-early gene and proxy for neural activity. Finally, we described the behavior and reproductive success of male IT-/- A. burtoni to evaluate the peptide’s role in male status and behavior. Our data show normal rates of typical social behaviors in both male and female IT-/- fish, as well as potential involvement of isotocin neurons in social descent. These results provide insight into the role of isotocin in female spawning behavior and male social status, and they also suggest possible signaling pathway redundancy.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
